# L §5.5 l.872, E §3.1 step 11 (tally 2.1.8 R13, #169-k): a cross-world diff is one ask, counted over the axes both
# worlds' cones reach: sa reads z through d, sb reads w, and the base x and y, so q reaches 4^4 = 256 combinations,
# past 64: LOAD-FORKS. Each world alone reaches 64 or fewer. 2.1.7's refcore printed every combination (exit 0).
=== base.tally
model fk
  tally 2
  on 2026-06-30
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "synthetic"

axis x
  x1 "1"
  x2 "2"
  x3 "3"
  x4 "4"

axis y
  y1 "1"
  y2 "2"
  y3 "3"
  y4 "4"

axis z
  z1 "1"
  z2 "2"
  z3 "3"
  z4 "4"

axis w
  w1 "1"
  w2 "2"
  w3 "3"
  w4 "4"

input flag: bool

def d: int at reader
  rule base = 1
  rule bx beats base = 0 when x == x2 and y == y2
  rule bz beats base, bx = 1 when flag

ask q: diff(d) in sa against sb law 2026-06-01

ask plain: d law 2026-06-01

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  flag = false
=== sa.tally
scenario sa over fk
  tally 2
  from 2026-01-01

version sa_v
  cite s
  governs 2026-01-01..
  known 2026-01-01
  replace rule d.bz = 1 when z == z2
=== sb.tally
scenario sb over fk
  tally 2
  from 2026-01-01

version sb_v
  cite s
  governs 2026-01-01..
  known 2026-01-01
  replace rule d.bz = 1 when w == w2
=== expect
refused: LOAD-FORKS ask q reaches 256 axis combinations; the cap is 64 (base.tally:40)
